<content>
  <section>
    <document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Add and Subtract Polynomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m81383</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Add and Subtract Polynomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c82f19a0-7d76-445d-a89f-9c230a8337f5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">In this lesson, you learned how to:</para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>Determine the degree of polynomials.</item>
        <item>Add and subtract polynomials.</item>
        <item>Evaluate a polynomial function for a given value.</item>
        <item>Add and subtract polynomial functions.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00002">Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that helped you reach those goals:</para>
      <list bullet-style="none" id="list-00002">
        <item>6.1.1: Understanding Polynomials</item>
        <item>
          <list id="list-00003">
            <item> In this activity, you classified different types of polynomials. You also found the degree of different polynomials.</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>6.1.2: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</item>
        <item>
          <list id="list-00004">
            <item> In this activity, you added and subtracted different types of polynomials by grouping like terms.</item>
            <item> 6.1.2: Self Check</item>
            <item>6.1.2: Additional Resources</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>6.1.3: Evaluating a Polynomial Function for a Given Value</item>
        <item>
          <list id="list-00005">
            <item> In this activity, you substituted given values into various polynomial functions and simplified to find solutions.</item>
            <item> 6.1.3: Self Check </item>
            <item>6.1.3: Additional Resources</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>6.1.4: Adding and Subtracting Polynomial Functions</item>
        <item>
          <list id="list-00006">
            <item>In this activity, you used the skills learned in previous activities to add and subtract polynomial functions. You were able to compare your work by evaluating the original and new polynomial functions using the same value.</item>
            <item> 6.1.4: Self Check </item>
            <item>6.1.4: Additional Resources</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>6.1.5: Reviewing Polynomial Addition and Subtraction</item>
        <item>
          <list id="list-00007">
            <item>In this activity, you reviewed all of the skills you learned adding and subtracting polynomials to solve a tic-tac-toe puzzle.</item>
          </list>
        </item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00003">After these activities, you completed the following <emphasis>practice</emphasis>:</para>
      <list bullet-style="none" id="list-00008">
        <item>6.1.6: Practice</item>
      </list>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00004">In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
      <para id="para-00005">A <term id="term-00001">monomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly one term: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
          <mrow>
            <mn>5</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>x</mi>
              <mn>2</mn>
            </msup>
          </mrow>
        </math></para>
      <para id="para-00006" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">A <term id="term-00002">binomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly two terms: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
          <mrow>
            <mn>5</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>x</mi>
              <mn>2</mn>
            </msup>
            <mo>+</mo>
            <mn>6</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>y</mi>
              <mn>3</mn>
            </msup>
          </mrow>
        </math></para>
      <para id="para-00007" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">A <term id="term-00003">trinomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly three terms: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
          <mrow>
            <mn>5</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>x</mi>
              <mn>2</mn>
            </msup>
            <mo>+</mo>
            <mn>6</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>y</mi>
              <mn>3</mn>
            </msup>
            <mo>−</mo>
            <mn>7</mn>
            <mi>c</mi>
          </mrow>
        </math></para>
      <para id="para-00008" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Monomials, binomials, and trinomials are all different types of polynomials. A <term id="term-00004"> polynomial</term> is a monomial or two or more monomials combined by addition or subtraction.</para>
      <para id="para-00009">The <term id="term-00005">degree of a polynomial</term> is the highest degree of all its terms.</para>
      <div class="os-raise-usermessage-link">
        <para id="para-00010">Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com"/>student.desmos.com using the information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00011">We will solve these two problems as a class.</para>
      <example id="exam-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated" id="list-00009">
              <item>Do these terms have the same variables with the same exponents? Explain your answer. <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>4</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>y</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>4</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>y</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00012" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00014">Yes. Both terms have the same variables raised to the same degrees: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mi>x</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math> is raised to the fourth power, and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mi>y</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math> is squared.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <example id="exam-00002" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <list type="enumerated" id="list-00010">
              <item> Add the terms together: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>4</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>y</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>4</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>y</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math>. What is the sum? </item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00015" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00017"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>14</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>x</mi>
                    <mn>4</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>y</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <para id="para-00018" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">In problems 3 - 7, simplify the monomial addition and subtraction problems on your own. Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>
      <!--Q#3-->
      <example id="exam-00003">
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <list type="enumerated" id="list-00011">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>12</mn>
                    <mi>q</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>9</mn>
                    <mi>q</mi>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00019" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00021"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>21</mn>
                  <mi>q</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <example id="exam-00004" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <list type="enumerated" id="list-00012">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>14</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>c</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>5</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>c</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00022" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00024"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>9</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>c</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <example id="exam-00005" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <list type="enumerated" id="list-00013">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>g</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>h</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>g</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>h</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00025" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00027"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>g</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>h</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      <example id="exam-00006" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <list type="enumerated" id="list-00014">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <mi>m</mi>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>n</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mi>p</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>5</mn>
                    <mi>m</mi>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>n</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mi>p</mi>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00028" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00030"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>13</mn>
                  <mi>m</mi>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>n</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mi>p</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <example id="exam-00007" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <list type="enumerated" id="list-00015">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>16</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>a</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>b</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>a</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00031" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00032">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00033"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>8</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>a</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>b</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <div class="os-raise-ib-input" data-button-text="Solution" data-content-id="52fdbc4d-81fd-4d07-a13e-40a06038bc34" data-fire-event="eventShow" data-schema-version="1.0" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-content">
          <list type="enumerated" id="list-00016">
            <item> Can the following monomials be simplified using like terms? Explain. <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>17</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>a</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>c</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>6</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>a</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>b</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>c</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></item>
          </list>
        </div>
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-prompt" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
          <para id="para-00034">Enter your answer here:</para>
        </div>
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-ack">
          <para id="para-00035">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00036">No, the monomial terms do not contain the same variables with the same exponents. The first term has the variable <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mi>b</mi>
              </mrow>
            </math> with an exponent of 1. The second term has the variable <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mi>b</mi>
              </mrow>
            </math> with an exponent of 2. The monomials are not like terms.</para>
        </div>
      </div>
      <para id="para-00037" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">In problems 9 - 13, simplify the polynomial addition and subtraction problems on your own. Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>
      <!--Q#9-->
      <example id="exam-00008">
        <exercise id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <list type="enumerated" id="list-00017">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>5</mn>
                    <mi>k</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>7</mn>
                    <mi>m</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>7</mn>
                    <mi>k</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>9</mn>
                    <mi>m</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>4</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00038" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">You can use the following video if you need some help.</para>
            <media alt="...">
              <iframe src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ba5bc330fb9f7f706e35cf5a38cdfedb85f34aec"/>
              <!-- <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On"
                      src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/712187a19365bfc0825bd798bf9f258c6ec2c971"
                      srclang="en_us">
                    </track> -->
            </media>
            <para id="para-00039">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00041"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>12</mn>
                  <mi>k</mi>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>16</mn>
                  <mi>m</mi>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>6</mn>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#10-->
      <example id="exam-00009" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated" id="list-00018">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>12</mn>
                    <mi>w</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>11</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>5</mn>
                    <mi>w</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00042" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00043">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00044"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>7</mn>
                  <mi>w</mi>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>5</mn>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#11-->
      <example id="exam-00010" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00010">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated" id="list-00019">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>9</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>h</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>4</mn>
                    <mi>h</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>17</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>h</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>9</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00045" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00046">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00047"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>11</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>h</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>4</mn>
                  <mi>h</mi>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>8</mn>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#12-->
      <example id="exam-00011" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00011">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated" id="list-00020">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>f</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <mi>f</mi>
                    <mi>v</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>v</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>f</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>v</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00048" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00049">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00050"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>f</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>6</mn>
                  <mi>f</mi>
                  <mi>v</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#13-->
      <example id="exam-00012" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00012">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="13" type="enumerated" id="list-00021">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>z</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <mi>z</mi>
                    <mi>x</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>12</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>z</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>4</mn>
                    <mi>z</mi>
                    <mi>x</mi>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00051" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00052">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00053"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>20</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>z</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>10</mn>
                  <mi>z</mi>
                  <mi>x</mi>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>x</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
      will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <example id="exam-00013" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
        <exercise id="exer-00013">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Start
      interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
      have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
      button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <div class="os-raise-ib-input" data-button-text="Solution" data-content-id="3cd1023e-3b4a-4e82-b3b2-ddd9101db872" data-schema-version="1.0" data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-content">
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para id="para-00054">Simplify. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
          <para id="para-00055"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>3</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
        </div>
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-prompt" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
          <para id="para-00056">Enter your answer here:</para>
        </div>
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-ack">
          <para id="para-00057">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00058"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
          <para id="para-00059" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">Here is how to solve this problem: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>3</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
          <para id="para-00060" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Distribute the multiplication over addition.</para>
          <para id="para-00061">
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
          <para id="para-00062" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Use the Commutative Property to rearrange like terms together.</para>
          <para id="para-00063">
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
          <para id="para-00064" xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml"><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Combine like terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00065">
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
    </section>
  </content>
  <glossary xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
    <definition id="fs-id1167836508735">
      <term>binomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167836508741">A binomial is a polynomial with exactly two terms.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167836508745">
      <term>degree of a constant</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167836508750">The degree of any constant is 0.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167836508755">
      <term>degree of a polynomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167836508760">The degree of a polynomial is the highest degree of all its terms.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167836508764">
      <term>degree of a term</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167833385664">The degree of a term is the sum of the exponents of its variables.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167833385668">
      <term>monomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167833385674">A monomial is an algebraic expression with one term. A monomial in one variable is a term of the form <m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
            </m:msup>
            <m:mo>,</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> where <emphasis effect="italics">a</emphasis> is a constant and <emphasis effect="italics">m</emphasis> is a whole number.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167829853744">
      <term>polynomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167829853749">A monomial or two or more monomials combined by addition or subtraction is a polynomial.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167829853754">
      <term>standard form of a polynomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167829853760">A polynomial is in standard form when the terms of a polynomial are written in descending order of degrees.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167833350539">
      <term>trinomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167833350544">A trinomial is a polynomial with exactly three terms.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167833350548">
      <term>polynomial function</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167833350554">A polynomial function is a function whose range values are defined by a polynomial.</meaning>
    </definition>
  </glossary>
</document>
  </section>
</content>